What is BST (Binary Search Tree)?

A BST is a special type of Binary Tree where:

Left Child  < Root
Right Child > Root


Example:

        50
       /  \
     30    70
    / \   / \
   20 40 60 80

Why BST?

Searching becomes faster because we can eliminate half the tree at every step.

Example: Search 60

60 > 50 → Go Right

60 < 70 → Go Left

Found 60


Time Complexity:

Search : O(log n)
Insert : O(log n)
Delete : O(log n)


(for balanced BST)

What is DFS (Depth First Search)?

DFS means:

Go as deep as possible first,
then come back.


DFS has 3 traversal types:

Preorder
Inorder
Postorder


Using the same tree:

        50
       /  \
     30    70
    / \   / \
   20 40 60 80

1. Preorder Traversal

Rule:

Root → Left → Right


Steps:

50
30
20
40
70
60
80


Output:

50 30 20 40 70 60 80

Easy Remember
Parent First
Then Children

2. Inorder Traversal

Rule:

Left → Root → Right


Steps:

20
30
40
50
60
70
80


Output:

20 30 40 50 60 70 80

Important Interview Point

For a BST:

Inorder Traversal
always produces
sorted output.


That's why interviewers frequently ask it.

3. Postorder Traversal

Rule:

Left → Right → Root


Steps:

20
40
30
60
80
70
50


Output:

20 40 30 60 80 70 50

Easy Remember
Parent Last

Quick Revision Table
Traversal	Rule	OutputPreorder	Root Left Right	50 30 20 40 70 60 80
Inorder	Left Root Right	20 30 40 50 60 70 80
Postorder	Left Right Root	20 40 30 60 80 70 50
Interview Answer (30 Seconds)

"A Binary Search Tree is a binary tree where all left nodes are smaller than the root and all right nodes are greater than the root.

DFS traversal has three types: Preorder (Root-Left-Right), Inorder (Left-Root-Right), and Postorder (Left-Right-Root). 
A key property is that Inorder traversal of a BST always gives elements in sorted order."

